 roughly 120 seconds (like the example)\n        Phase2: Multi-length resampling (300 seconds)\n        Phase3: Restart mechanisms (anytime, self-looped)\n\n   But note: we are not required to use the same phases as the example.\n\n   A fallback sequence is an initialization of what? The previous program assumes a normalized sum and a minimization to the best score.\n\n   We'll return the sequence after the primary optimization. We must ensure the sequence sum is above 0.01.\n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**\n   The returned sequence is a list of floats, nonnegative. We must validate:\n\n        The sum is above 0.01 -> otherwise, we revert after the evaluator returns inf.\n\n   The evaluation function:\n\n        Returns inf if sum(a) < 0.01\n\n   We also need to protect against negative values and numbers too large.\n\n   The best candidate is stored during the search and then takes over if no improvement in a new candidate is found.\n\n   The algorithm will try to use the evaluator's defensive checks to its advantage:\n\n        If a candidate has a sum less than 0.01 or contains negative numbers, we'll resample and skip.\n\n   This is a permissive approach, but the coder should not rely on it to 'fix' a candidate.\n\n   We'll now write the detailed implementation for the coder.",
